{
	"nodes":[
		{"id":"d660e1160b9c1bf9","type":"text","text":"Idea #1","x":-310,"y":-37,"width":250,"height":60,"color":"1"},
		{"id":"5bce9a83cec71437","type":"text","text":"Pitch: Your on your way to work when you get kidnapped. You wake up on an island with 50 other people tied to a chair surrounding a big bonfire. There are people in front of each of you in dark red robes. They explain to you the situation. You must survive here for 50 days with 50 people. They kill someone on the spot to let us understand the severity of the situation. They untie you and disperse, revealing a ton of weapons and resources surrounding the fire. You rush to get as many weapons as you can before people start shooting. A backpack will always be available to us to take, players choice whether he wants the backpack or not (players who really want a challenge can skip the backpack). With the weapons you have gathered, you must survive for 50 days. In that time, you can make alliances. In alliances you can share weapons and resources, and generally help each other out. But there is no such thing as loyalty, no contract, no guarantee they won't stab you in the back. There are benefits to alliances that aren't available to lone wolves, but there are also risks. The player won't have a screen showing the NPC's characteristics, its up to us to determine whether we want to trust them or not. For mechanics, breathing and blinking will be manual. Blinking too much will cause you to miss information (an enemy running by), but not blinking enough will cause you to hallucinate and see things (enemies, resources) that aren't there. Breathing too fast will quicken recovery, but will be louder and cause aim to shake. Slow breathing will cause recovery to be slower, but increase aim accuracy, and be quieter to avoid alerting enemies. Scattered around the island are a ton of metal materials. If you discover the hidden underground bunker, you can use the materials to hack into their system. This will allow the user to finally be able to see NPC characteristics, though only in this location, and it takes time so alliance members may get suspicious if your gone too long. Suspicion within the alliance decreases loyalty. If anyone follows you to the hidden bunker, the next time you come, the computer/servers will be broken. Though the chance is low, if you are injured and lose a body part, you will be unable to use the mechanics tied to that body part. There is a bleeding out period where your losing blood, where you can heal yourself if you have the equipment necessary, or get healed by an alliance member. For example if you lose one eye, your depth perception is off, causing either blur when aiming, or increased inaccuracy. If you lose an arm, the weight/size of the things you can carry is halved. If you lose your left arm, reloading will take longer. If you lose your right arm, your aim accuracy is halved. Losing a leg will cause your walking speed to be very slow, unless you stick a stick into the wound to use as a prosthetic. Losing your tongue will disable the ability for new alliances, old ones stay. Each game will be randomized. Random NPCs with random characteristics, random weapons, random attacks etc. The NPCs also have the ability for alliances, but this will be more random compared to the players. They can follow you, steal from you, attack you. You must set traps, deter, or kill them first. If your being shot at, you can grab dead NPCs (or even alive injured ones) and use them as human shields.  But doing so will increase hostility island wide, and increase the chance of attacks from whatever alliance's person you used as a shield. There aren't enough resources for everyone, so if you want to survive, you must take. If you survive till the end (your the last one standing, or you find and kill all of the cultists), you will have a choice to leave the island on a boat, or to check out the secret base of the cult. The boat is the normal ending, the base contains a book. when you go through it, it shows the name of each person and how they died. They were predicting deaths. This adds to lore. Lore is to be determined. If you, or the NPCs haven't attacked each other in a while, you all start working together, the cultists will come out at night, and kill some of you. This builds distrust among members and raises tension. So kill or be killed. The cult is actually a bunch of scientists, or something like that, funded by the government, to run these simulations to feed databases to predict how humans will behave when resources are limited and laws are dissolved. We find this out from tiny clues throughout the game. Doing immoral things like using someone who's alive as a shield will affect your mental health, by causing blinking time to be longer, and breathing to become more irregular. Blinking and breathing can happen on its own, but it is optional for the player to do it manually. This becomes useful during the game and specific cases. You must contribute to the alliance if you want loyalty. They will track how much resources you contribute, if you are actively participating in attacks, if you choose to use your own resources to save an alliance member, or choose to let them die. The core theme of this game is that the body, and morality degrade under time and pressure. ","x":-620,"y":150,"width":870,"height":1150},
